1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,673
Green velvet on the compound wall.

2
00:00:02,673 --> 00:00:04,761
A soft cushion on a damp step.

3
00:00:04,761 --> 00:00:07,330
Flat green plates beside a dripping tap.

4
00:00:07,730 --> 00:00:09,382
All three are bryophytes.

5
00:00:09,382 --> 00:00:12,316
And all three turn bright
green when it rains.

6
00:00:12,766 --> 00:00:16,815
Diya noticed the velvet moss on
her compound wall every monsoon.

7
00:00:16,815 --> 00:00:19,101
In summer it turns dry and brown.

8
00:00:19,101 --> 00:00:22,254
She asked: why does it
need the rain so much?

9
00:00:22,604 --> 00:00:27,032
Bryophytes live on land, but they need
water to make the next generation.

10
00:00:27,032 --> 00:00:30,896
That is why they are called the
amphibians of the plant kingdom.

11
00:00:31,396 --> 00:00:35,832
By the end, you will know the body of
a bryophyte, how it reproduces, and

12
00:00:35,832 --> 00:00:38,123
how to tell a liverwort from a moss.

13
00:00:38,773 --> 00:00:40,991
So what does a bryophyte look like?

14
00:00:41,341 --> 00:00:42,579
Its body is simple.

15
00:00:42,579 --> 00:00:45,682
It may lie flat on the
ground, or stand up straight.

16
00:00:46,132 --> 00:00:50,339
It holds on with rhizoids, thin
threads made of one cell or many.

17
00:00:50,339 --> 00:00:54,891
It has no true roots, stem or leaves,
only parts that look like them.

18
00:00:55,341 --> 00:00:57,540
The green plant you see is haploid.

19
00:00:57,540 --> 00:01:00,485
That means its cells have
one set of chromosomes.

20
00:01:00,485 --> 00:01:03,802
It makes the gametes, so it
is called the gametophyte.

21
00:01:04,452 --> 00:01:08,241
Now let's zoom into the velvet
on the wall, one level at a time.

22
00:01:08,591 --> 00:01:11,676
From far away, it is a
green cushion on the bricks.

23
00:01:12,176 --> 00:01:13,076
Zoom in.

24
00:01:12,963 --> 00:01:17,374
The cushion is hundreds of tiny
moss plants, packed side by side.

25
00:01:17,874 --> 00:01:18,859
Zoom again.

26
00:01:18,859 --> 00:01:20,121
One moss plant.

27
00:01:20,121 --> 00:01:24,785
An upright axis, leaves arranged
in a spiral, and rhizoids below.

28
00:01:24,785 --> 00:01:27,492
On top grows a stalk with a capsule.

29
00:01:27,992 --> 00:01:29,537
Zoom into the capsule.

30
00:01:29,537 --> 00:01:31,091
It is full of spores.

31
00:01:31,091 --> 00:01:33,549
Each spore can grow into a new moss.

32
00:01:34,199 --> 00:01:37,209
How does a bryophyte
make the next generation?

33
00:01:37,209 --> 00:01:38,947
Watch it in five stages.

34
00:01:39,297 --> 00:01:40,206
Stage one.

35
00:01:40,206 --> 00:01:42,191
The plant grows sex organs.

36
00:01:42,191 --> 00:01:44,499
The male one is the antheridium.

37
00:01:44,499 --> 00:01:49,815
The female one is the archegonium,
shaped like a flask, with one egg inside.

38
00:01:50,565 --> 00:01:51,512
Stage two.

39
00:01:51,512 --> 00:01:56,289
The antheridium releases antherozoids,
male gametes with two flagella.

40
00:01:56,289 --> 00:01:59,424
They need water to swim
to the archegonium.

41
00:02:00,174 --> 00:02:01,227
Stage three.

42
00:02:01,227 --> 00:02:03,791
One antherozoid fuses with the egg.

43
00:02:03,791 --> 00:02:05,409
This makes a zygote.

44
00:02:06,159 --> 00:02:07,059
Stage four.

45
00:02:07,006 --> 00:02:11,264
The zygote grows into the sporophyte:
a foot, a stalk called the seta,

46
00:02:11,264 --> 00:02:12,304
and a capsule.

47
00:02:12,304 --> 00:02:16,195
It stays attached to the green
plant and takes its food from it.

48
00:02:16,945 --> 00:02:17,970
Stage five.

49
00:02:17,970 --> 00:02:22,884
Inside the capsule, cells divide
by meiosis and make haploid spores.

50
00:02:22,884 --> 00:02:25,926
Each spore grows into a new green plant.

51
00:02:26,676 --> 00:02:27,663
Remember this.

52
00:02:27,663 --> 00:02:31,313
In a bryophyte, the green plant
you see is the gametophyte.

53
00:02:31,913 --> 00:02:35,456
Bryophytes come in two
kinds: liverworts and mosses.

54
00:02:35,806 --> 00:02:40,208
A liverwort, like Marchantia, is a
flat green body pressed close to the

55
00:02:40,208 --> 00:02:41,108
ground.

56
00:02:41,293 --> 00:02:44,032
It also makes new plants without gametes.

57
00:02:44,032 --> 00:02:49,096
Small cups on its surface, called gemma
cups, hold green buds called gemmae.

58
00:02:49,096 --> 00:02:52,217
A gemma falls off and
grows into a new plant.

59
00:02:52,817 --> 00:02:56,911
A moss starts from a spore as the
protonema: a green, creeping, branched

60
00:02:56,911 --> 00:02:57,811
thread.

61
00:02:57,932 --> 00:03:02,712
A bud on it grows into the leafy
stage, the upright plant that bears the

62
00:03:02,712 --> 00:03:03,658
sex organs.

63
00:03:03,658 --> 00:03:06,701
Funaria, Polytrichum
and Sphagnum are mosses.

64
00:03:07,301 --> 00:03:08,277
Your turn.

65
00:03:08,277 --> 00:03:12,148
A flat green plate has tiny
cups holding green buds.

66
00:03:12,148 --> 00:03:13,641
Liverwort or moss?

67
00:03:13,641 --> 00:03:15,077
Pause and decide.

68
00:03:18,277 --> 00:03:19,177
Liverwort!

69
00:03:19,115 --> 00:03:23,478
Gemma cups with gemmae are a sign
of a liverwort like Marchantia.

70
00:03:24,128 --> 00:03:26,030
Small plants, big jobs.

71
00:03:26,380 --> 00:03:31,099
Mosses and lichens are among the first
to grow on bare rock, slowly turning

72
00:03:31,099 --> 00:03:32,174
it into soil.

73
00:03:32,174 --> 00:03:35,040
Moss mats stop rain
from washing soil away.

74
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:39,550
And Sphagnum gives peat, used as
fuel and as packing, because it holds

75
00:03:39,550 --> 00:03:40,674
so much water.

76
00:03:41,324 --> 00:03:43,084
Now, a worked example.

77
00:03:43,084 --> 00:03:44,884
Gametophyte or sporophyte?

78
00:03:44,884 --> 00:03:46,758
Let's sort six moss parts.

79
00:03:47,108 --> 00:03:49,507
The leafy shoot is the gametophyte.

80
00:03:49,507 --> 00:03:51,021
So is the protonema.

81
00:03:51,471 --> 00:03:54,885
A gemma of a liverwort is
part of the gametophyte too.

82
00:03:55,335 --> 00:03:58,442
The foot, the seta and the
capsule are the sporophyte.

83
00:03:58,442 --> 00:04:00,017
They grow from the zygote.

84
00:04:00,667 --> 00:04:02,133
Now, the exam trap.

85
00:04:02,483 --> 00:04:05,870
Many students think the green
moss plant is the sporophyte.

86
00:04:06,270 --> 00:04:07,750
It is the gametophyte.

87
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:12,299
The sporophyte is only the foot, seta
and capsule, growing on top of it.

88
00:04:12,949 --> 00:04:14,314
Where is this asked?

89
00:04:14,314 --> 00:04:18,528
As statements on the gametophyte,
the sporophyte, gemmae and protonema.

90
00:04:19,128 --> 00:04:20,345
Exam level now.

91
00:04:20,345 --> 00:04:22,140
Read the four statements.

92
00:04:22,140 --> 00:04:23,831
Which ones are correct?

93
00:04:23,831 --> 00:04:25,100
Pause and solve.

94
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:31,781
The answer is B.

95
00:04:31,781 --> 00:04:33,509
Statements two and three.

96
00:04:34,009 --> 00:04:35,095
One is wrong.

97
00:04:35,095 --> 00:04:38,202
The main plant body of
a bryophyte is haploid.

98
00:04:38,702 --> 00:04:39,944
Four is wrong.

99
00:04:39,944 --> 00:04:43,371
Gemmae are asexual buds,
not sexual structures.

100
00:04:43,971 --> 00:04:45,430
Here is your recap.

101
00:04:45,430 --> 00:04:46,930
Screenshot this card.

102
00:04:50,130 --> 00:04:52,966
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

103
00:04:53,366 --> 00:04:54,266
One.

104
00:04:53,917 --> 00:04:56,906
Why do bryophytes need water to reproduce?

105
00:04:57,806 --> 00:04:58,706
Two.

106
00:04:58,264 --> 00:05:01,035
What are the three parts
of a moss sporophyte?

107
00:05:01,935 --> 00:05:02,835
Three.

108
00:05:02,526 --> 00:05:04,165
Where are gemmae formed?

109
00:05:05,265 --> 00:05:08,537
Next time: brown dots
under the leaves of a fern.

110
00:05:08,537 --> 00:05:09,875
Is the plant sick?

111
00:05:09,875 --> 00:05:11,800
We find out in pteridophytes.
